            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I see (a little) mounting pressure on this, and I have an idea of how to implement it, but I don't have the time for the next 1-2 months.\n\nFor now, I'll announce a design I settled on in case it inspires others:\n\n## proposal: vim.fs.watch\n\n`vim.fs.watch(patterns, opts, handler)` subscribes to one `fd`-handler-style glob/list of globs. nvim then resolves the patterns, shares the necessary OS watchers, filters and coalesces their events, and invokes `handler(path, change)` with the matching path. The watcher owns this logical subscription and is responsible for closing itself with `watcher:close()`.\n\n### Context\n\nToday, autoread can create one physical watcher per file - a good start - but obviusly creates problems like #40238. A shared broker that handles similar capabilities wanted in LSPs, autoread, and plugins is desirable. With vim.fs.watch, a feature creates a logical subscription and the underlying watcher layer/broker chooses the physical arrangement. Enjoy this AI diagram:\n\n```text\nautoread          /repo/lua/init.lua\nFugitive          /repo/** and /repo/.git/{HEAD,index,refs/**}\ndirectory buffer  /repo/*\nLSP               /repo/**/*.rs\n                         │\n                         ▼\n               shared vim.fs.watch registry\n                         │\n                         ▼\n              a small set of native OS watches\n```\n\nIf several subscriptions are covered by the same directory watcher, reuse it and dispatches matching paths to each handler. The watcher  broker/registry-thingy owns *everything*: refcounting, recovery, and more. \n\n### Examples\n\nThis auto-updates tpope/vim-fugitive on external changes to the fs.\n\n```lua\nlocal worktree = vim.fn.FugitiveWorkTree(buf)\nlocal gitdir = vim.fn.FugitiveCommonDir(buf)\n\nlocal watcher = vim.fs.watch({\n  worktree .. '/**',\n  gitdir .. '/{HEAD,index,refs/**}',\n}, {\n  exclude = { worktree .. '/.git/objects/**' },\n  debounce = 100,\n}, function(_path, _change)\n  -- Not a very illumating example: change will contain helpful metadata\n  vim.schedule(function()\n    if vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(buf) then\n      vim.fn.FugitiveDidChange(buf)\n    end\n  end)\nend)\n\nvim.api.nvim_buf_attach(buf, false, {\n  on_detach = function()\n    watcher:close()\n  end,\n})\n```\n",
